button history. Preserve editor input during pending search. A qualifying
reset finishes recipe, quantity and incremental drag through their existing
request owners and original source identities, without retiring the menu.
Preserve empty-primary bundle hover, clear admitted selected-source context,
and retain the local selection through unchanged snapshot publications.
Keep arrow and scrollbar controller admission guarded pending their owners.

Honor the original held-input edge filters independently of activation.
Return press requires raw phase one after another phase. Return release
requires raw phase zero after phase one. Unmatched and intermediate
releases preserve the held flag, timer and source until a matching edge.
Original 1.26.60.5 producer/dispatcher controls cover all three modes,
phases and previous phases. Real-menu regressions keep quantity selection
active after unmatched releases, then complete once through its owner.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 runs a separate exact-source hover-exit callback after bundle hover cleanup. Retire ordinary and touch hover even without an active desktop tooltip, so later item updates cannot reopen a tooltip for a departed slot. Preserve the earlier desktop callback's collection-alias cleanup and the independent primary and touch bundle sources.

Retain selected-source cooking commands in the same client input sequence
as recipes, bundles, search and held quantities. Bind pending replies to
their complete original action, preserve drawing ownership across newer
item frames, and wait for replacement tap authority when core retires the
old frame. Reentrant replies cannot dispatch another command mid-send.
Keep a typed terminal retirement after ambiguous drawing transport; it
retains the original source frame and cannot release a later gesture.
Register source capability and result transport independently of the
selected input mode.

Retain the persistent bundle hover flag from raw arrow pressed edges in
touch mode, publish it to the authored scene and expire it at six seconds.
Repeated pressed phases and arrow release do not restart or cancel it.
A real input-mode change clears it. Valid source pre-press resets the flag
before item admission; absent contexts preserve it.

Original Bedrock 1.26.60.5 callback, getter and frame controls establish the
timer boundary. Eighteen original pre-press controls establish the reset
with item lookup supplied and execution stopped before item validity.
Regressions check visible state, repeated input and unchanged-frame revision.
Keep default cooking admission guarded until its remaining callbacks have
real consumers. This does not establish physical touch or complete screen parity.

Connect selected-source cooking input to its snapshot-bound command owner.
Selection retains its ordinary slot and keeps the cursor empty. The first
drawing event retains a destination; the second begins source distribution
with that destination before its current cell. Later visits and completion
use the ordered incremental request path without cursor-drag Start input or
Java menu prediction. Authority owns destination eligibility and revisits.

An explicit cooking control style keeps Mouse and keyboard as the default
and permits Touch-style contact through held mouse input. Hover without
contact cannot distribute items. Keep contact alive through the final slot
release before retiring hover. This is a declared Java input policy, not
evidence of physical touchscreen hardware support.

Preserve native arrow entry blocking and the shared scrollbar-touch and
cursor-drop-all flag. A valid ordinary pre-press resets the shared blocker.
Source selection uses the released cell, including an empty cell. Native
release activation also completes drawing after component mapping history
was invalidated. Rejected admission clears local drawing ownership before
new input can use it.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 component, controller and original state-machine controls
establish ordinary selection, tap routing, retained destinations, arrow
blocking and release behavior for hotbar, inventory and furnace sources.
Six release-target controls retain the released cell without moving items;
six drawing controls complete with previous component phases one and two.
Source lookup, permissions, item services, synchronous atomic mutation,
clock and runtime services remain supplied boundaries. These controls do
not establish native multiplayer grouping or physical touch delivery.

Regressions use the real client action owner for pending and rejected
admission, shared flags and tap quantities. A screen fixture drives mouse entry points and
checks contact, real authored collection details, source-payload identity,
ordered drawing clicks and release without Java pickup or prediction.
Touch bundle branches remain unfinished. Keep default cooking host
admission guarded.

Keep initial secondary input on the release phase. Six additional native
controls distinguish ordinary half pickup without shape admission from
source selection after shape admission. Retain that distinction through
the real mouse event path: a shaped secondary contact selects without an
ordinary Java pickup, while an unshaped secondary release delegates one
ordinary pickup to the existing inventory owner. The native legacy router
and source post-update execute; atomic count, capacity and mutation remain
supplied model services. These controls do not establish multiplayer
request grouping or complete touch input parity.

Original Bedrock 1.26.60.5 controller instructions establish Touch quantity
selection without cursor pickup. Option600 initializes its trigger to
525 milliseconds. Admission is strictly after that threshold, excludes
single-item sources and retains the existing secondary-source identity.
Pointer movement uses the native float sine curve and truncated count.
Release freezes that fraction and retains the source for a later tap or
drawing budget. Neither selection nor release submits an item request.
The later transfer uses the existing snapshot-bound authority owner.

Native frame, state-machine, getter and tap controls cover threshold,
movement cancellation, GUI scalar, release retention and a three-of-nine
transfer. Clock, pointer, option lookup, GUI scalar, CRT sine and model
services remain declared boundaries. These controls do not establish
physical touchscreen input, presentation pixels or multiplayer grouping.
Client regressions exercise physical screen callbacks, partial drawing,
ordered completion and budget retirement without Java item prediction.
The client currently uses the native default trigger. Configurable trigger
time and Touch bundle branches remain unfinished.

Render retained Touch source images through the authored selected-item
controls instead of treating every selected image as the real cursor.
Publish an immutable source/count display snapshot to the shared controller.
Use actual menu components with the selected quantity, and leave both the
source slot and real cursor unchanged. Reuse unchanged presentation data
rather than rebuilding native item metadata on every pointer frame.

Selected-image parents use the shared native cursor-following layout.
Their icons and count labels keep authored bounds, visibility, layer and
opacity. Ordinary carried items keep their existing extraction owner.
Give the renderer a copy of the selected image so item extraction cannot
mutate the retained display snapshot. Retire the image when source
selection ends, without an inventory action.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 ui_common.json supplies the selected icon, count and parent
controls. Original target-build source/count and pointer-curve controls
establish the retained quantity with an empty cursor. Real screen tests
resolve those authored templates, exercise a seven-of-nine image and count,
retain physical slot hit regions, and check extraction opacity and snapshot
isolation through release and drawing completion. Native pixels, details
popup, Touch bundles and physical touchscreen delivery remain unfinished.

Admit the registered arrow and scrollbar state callbacks through their
existing source owners. Keep scroll-view admission limited to the authored
scrollbar callback, and preserve unsupported and nonconsuming host guards.
Deliver scrollbar press bookkeeping before pointer capture, so a held
scrollbar cannot start source hover quantity selection over an item.

Forward raw arrow aliases alongside ordinary keyboard focus input and
match their releases, including input bookkeeping while replies wait.
The target-build default panels name these aliases. Original 1.26.60.5
producer, dispatcher and callback controls establish the held flags, and a
read-only native Right-arrow control observes their press/release lifetime.
Screen regressions exercise the authored persistent-text binding through
expiry and the scrollbar-to-item hold boundary without item movement.
Exact native scheduling and graphical parity remain unverified. Default
program admission still stops at the unsupported coalesce_stack action.

Admit screen-level cursor drops in a count-one scope. Every chest screen
inherits button.cursor_drop_all and button.cursor_drop_one from
common.inventory_screen_common, and CubeCraft's Profile and Skyblock menus
are count-one scopes, so rejecting every cursor drop left them in the
generic chest. Count-one actions never fill the cursor, so a drop has no
item there. A server-set carried item leaves the scope for the stock
screen, because the count-one transport cannot drop or place it. A
cursor drop on an item cell is still rejected. Native outside-drop
behavior with a server-set cursor item is unmeasured.

Present logical containers. The Java menu of a 549-slot shop is the
54-slot chest, so the scene reads container slots from the logical items
sent for that presentation, keyed by full slot, and its player slots from
the menu. Logical slots are read-only Java slots: they render items,
counts and tooltips, but vanilla pickup and placement never reach them.
A logical presentation needs native actions, its first item reset and a
count-one scope; otherwise the ordinary chest stays. A click sends the
full logical slot, for example 487, through the count-one action.

Show the scene's hover text renderer as the container tooltip. Bedrock
draws an item tooltip through a common.hover_text control, so a pack's
hover text binding decides its contents. CubeCraft's Skyblock Quest button
binds it to the item's hover text after its first 700 characters, which
hold the progress ratio, flag and button label padded with backticks. The
Java tooltip showed the whole converted name and lore, including that
control text. Official 1.26.60.5 shows only the remaining text. When no
renderer with text is shown, as for an item without retained native hover
text, the Java item tooltip still stands in.

Resolve native container, cooking and player-inventory presentation from the confirmed UI generation. Messages retain authoritative menu, item, recipe and revision state but carry no UI program. Reject stale generations while retaining ordinary screens and existing action ownership. Source-built programs retain the native hurt-policy contract.

Give the six-row synthetic container screen a declared six-row grid and matching pixel height. A fixed 9x3 grid now correctly stops at 27 entries under the native capacity contract. Keep menu identity, full coverage and actual click dispatch assertions for every storage family.

Keep the cooking binding lifetime across accepted snapshot revisions.
Refresh the title through the same retained state. A new opening or UI
context gets a fresh view. Core ONCE bindings then survive item and
progress updates instead of restarting at every catalog snapshot.
The presentation regression checks count changes, title refresh, another
opening and a replacement UI context through the production resolver.

Match variable-size actor presentations to the Java row count derived from their complete metadata size. CubeCraft Profile now advertises 36 slots; retaining the six-row-only admission rule would reject its corrected four-row menu. Keep the existing six-row prefix for inventories above Java capacity and reject mismatched menu generations or dimensions.

Derive the logical renderer's player-slot boundary from the actual Java prefix as well as checking the menu rows. The installed 36-slot Profile exposed a remaining fixed-54 constructor guard and offset that disconnected on contents. Keep player inventory and hotbar slots after the four-row prefix, including partially filled rows; retain the existing high-slot prefix above Java capacity.
